WHAT YOU ALREADY KNOW   What you already know
Chapter 1 defined operational risk from four sources and financial risk in four kinds. Chapter 5 supplied the five responses. Book 1 measured Orontes's receivables at 36 days and its cash at $7,302,100; Book 2 hedged a sesame price with a forward contract. This chapter is the method behind all of them.
7.1  Managing operational risk
Operational risk is managed almost entirely by reduction, because, as Chapter 1 noted, nobody is paid a return for bearing it. The methods fall into six groups and an answer that uses the groups is clearer than one that lists twenty controls.
Controls, in their two kinds. A preventive control stops the event — an approval before a payment, a check before a delivery is accepted, an access right that does not exist. A detective control finds it afterwards — a reconciliation, an exception report, a stock count, a review of unusual items. Both are needed: prevention is better and never complete, and detection limits the duration of what prevention missed.
Structural controls. Segregation of duties, so that no one person both performs and checks a transaction; authorisation limits that rise with seniority; mandatory leave, during which somebody else does the job and anything being concealed surfaces; rotation of duties; dual signatures on payments. These are the controls that address deliberate as well as accidental loss, and Chapter 14's fraud triangle explains why they work.
Standardisation and automation. A documented procedure performed the same way every time produces fewer errors than judgment exercised afresh; a system that will not accept an invalid entry prevents what a reminder cannot. The caution of Chapter 5 applies: automation converts many small human errors into few large systematic ones, so an automated control needs its own testing.
People measures. Recruitment checks, training, supervision, competence assessment, succession planning for key roles, and attention to workload and turnover, which are among the best leading indicators of operational failure.
Redundancy and resilience. A second supplier, a standby machine, backups kept somewhere else, spare capacity, cross-trained staff. These do not reduce the probability of an event; they reduce what it costs, which section 7.2 develops.
Measurement and feedback. A loss database that records events and near-misses, root cause analysis of significant ones, key risk indicators of the kind Chapter 6 listed, and the postaudit discipline of asking, after every loss, which control should have caught it and why it did not.
WORKED EXAMPLE   Worked example: designing the control for one process
Step 1. The process. Orontes pays about four hundred supplier invoices a month. The risks are paying an invoice that is not owed, paying it twice, paying the wrong amount, and paying a fraudulent supplier invented by an employee.
Step 2. Preventive controls. Three-way matching of purchase order, goods received note and invoice, performed by the system; a supplier master file that only two named people may change, with changes reviewed by a third; payment authorisation limits by value; and bank details verified by telephone to a number held on file, never to a number on the invoice.
Step 3. Detective controls. A monthly report of duplicate invoice numbers and amounts; a quarterly review of new suppliers created, matched against the employee address file; a supplier statement reconciliation.
Step 4. The structural control. The person who creates a supplier may not approve a payment to it, and the person who approves may not release it at the bank.
Check. Notice the shape. Four risks, ten controls, and the costly ones are the automated preventive checks that run on every transaction. The structural control costs nothing but organisation, and it is the one most often absent in a small finance team — which is precisely why the fake-supplier fraud of Chapter 12 is so common in small finance teams.
