7.4  Managing liquidity risk
Chapter 2 established that liquidity failure is what actually ends companies. The management methods are four.
Forecasting. A rolling cash forecast — thirteen weeks in detail, twelve months in outline — built from receipts and payments rather than from profit, with the sensitivity of Book 3's Chapter 4 applied to the two or three assumptions that matter. A company that cannot say what its cash will be in eight weeks is not managing liquidity; it is watching it.
Buffers. Cash and committed facilities sized against something, not chosen round. A defensible basis is the fixed cost base: Orontes's fixed costs of $12,624,000 are 12,624,000 ÷ 12 = 1,052,000 a month, so three months of fixed costs with no receipts at all is 1,052,000 × 3 = 3,156,000 — which the $7,302,100 cash balance covers twice over, and which gives the board a reason for the number rather than a habit.
Structure. Matching the maturity of funding to the life of the assets it supports, so that long assets are not financed by money that can be withdrawn; staggering maturities so that nothing large falls due at once; keeping covenant headroom, which Chapter 6 measured at 4,348,800 − 1,560,000 = 2,788,800 of operating income; and holding facilities that are committed rather than uncommitted.
Working capital management. Everything in Book 1's Chapters 11 to 13: collecting faster, paying no earlier than agreed, holding less stock. These are the cheapest liquidity sources a company has, and the 690,408 released by six days of collection in section 7.3 is larger than most facilities a company of Orontes's size would negotiate.
