LANGUAGE FOCUS   "Control", "hedge" and "exposure"
Control was discussed in Chapter 4; here note the pair preventive and detective, which in English are adjectives describing when the control acts rather than how strong it is — a detective control is not a weak one, it is a later one. Hedge comes from the hedgerow that marks a boundary and limits movement, and the metaphor is exact: a hedge does not improve the position, it narrows the range of outcomes in both directions. Candidates who write that a hedge protects against loss have given half the definition, and the other half — that it gives up the gain — is what makes hedging a policy decision rather than an obvious good. Arabic التحوط carries the same idea of putting something around a thing for protection; French couverture is a covering. And exposure, which Chapter 1 defined, is the word to use when you mean the amount at stake: an exposure is hedged, a risk is managed, and a loss is incurred, and keeping the three verbs with their proper nouns makes a report much easier to read.
