Chapter summary
Figure F807-03 brings the chapter together.
Figure F807-03. Chapter 7 summary map: the key rule for each section.
Operational risk is managed by reduction, through preventive and detective controls, structural controls such as segregation of duties and mandatory leave, standardisation and automation, people measures, redundancy, and measurement through loss data and indicators — with automation needing its own testing because it turns many small errors into few systematic ones. Continuity planning handles what cannot be prevented, distinguishing the business continuity plan from the disaster recovery plan and resting on a business impact analysis, a recovery time objective, a recovery point objective and a critical dependency list, with testing, accessibility and named people as the three rules that make it real. Credit risk is managed along the life of a receivable — assess, structure, monitor, collect, and limit concentration — and priced rather than avoided: 600,000 × 0.02 = 12,000 of defaulted exposure at a 70 per cent loss given default is 12,000 × 0.70 = 8,400 against 240,000 of contribution, while six days off the collection period on 42,000,000 ÷ 365 = 115,068 of daily sales releases 115,068 × 6 = 690,408. Liquidity is managed by rolling forecasts, buffers sized against something — 12,624,000 ÷ 12 = 1,052,000 a month of fixed costs, so 1,052,000 × 3 = 3,156,000 for a quarter — maturity structure and covenant headroom, and working capital. And market risk is managed in order of cost: natural hedges first, operational responses second, instruments third and governance throughout, so that a one-point rate rise costing 6,500,000 × 0.01 = 65,000 is left open deliberately while a net euro exposure of 2,000,000 − 400,000 = 1,600,000 is hedged to policy at 1,600,000 × 0.75 = 1,200,000.
