Case-style practice: the new distributor
A new distributor in a neighbouring country offers Orontes 2,400,000 of annual sales at a contribution margin of 40 per cent. It wants ninety-day terms and payment in euros. Its credit agency rating implies a 4 per cent probability of default, and Orontes would expect to recover 30 per cent of any unpaid balance. Chapter 6's tolerances are 600,000 for a single distributor and 2,500,000 for export receivables in total, with at least 75 per cent of contracted currency exposure hedged.
Required. Evaluate the proposal against the tolerances, quantify the credit and currency exposures, and recommend terms on which it could be accepted. Under 350 words.
Answers and explanations are at the end of the chapter.
Answers and explanations
Section checks
SC7-1  Answer A. It finds errors after the event.
B is wrong. Prevention acts before.
C is wrong. Structural controls concern who does what.
D is wrong. Nothing has been moved to another party.
SC7-2  Answer A. Concealment requires continuous presence.
B is wrong. Workload is a side effect.
C is wrong. It costs rather than saves.
D is wrong. Legal requirements vary and are not the mechanism.
SC7-3  Answer A. Systematic errors replace scattered ones.
B is wrong. Automated controls need more testing, not less.
C is wrong. It changes the shape of the risk.
D is wrong. Cost depends on the case.
SC7-4  Answer A. Systems and data are its scope.
B is wrong. Continuity covers the whole business.
C is wrong. Insurance is a transfer.
D is wrong. Assessment precedes planning.
SC7-5  Answer A. The cost of interruption is a business fact.
B is wrong. Technology delivers it; it does not set it.
C is wrong. Insurers price the consequence.
D is wrong. Software is a constraint, not an objective.
SC7-6  Answer A. The unmapped dependency is the usual failure.
B is wrong. Length is a symptom at worst.
C is wrong. Insurance does not keep a business running.
D is wrong. Quantification helps but is not the failure.
SC7-7  Answer A. 12,000 × 0.70 = 8,400.
B is wrong. That is the defaulted exposure before recovery.
C is wrong. That applies the loss rate to the whole balance.
D is wrong. That is the exposure.
SC7-8  Answer A. 42,000,000 ÷ 365 = 115,068.
B is wrong. That is annual jar volume.
C is wrong. That misplaces a decimal.
D is wrong. That is ten times too small.
SC7-9  Answer A. A limit and terms, not a binary answer.
B is wrong. That throws away the middle ground.
C is wrong. A claim follows a loss.
D is wrong. A provision follows an expectation of loss.
SC7-10  Answer A. 12,624,000 ÷ 12 = 1,052,000.
B is wrong. That is the annual figure.
C is wrong. That is ten times too small.
D is wrong. That is a quarter.
SC7-11  Answer A. 1,052,000 × 3 = 3,156,000.
B is wrong. That is one month.
C is wrong. That is a year.
D is wrong. That is operating income.
SC7-12  Answer A. Long assets, long money.
B is wrong. Early payment worsens liquidity.
C is wrong. More inventory consumes cash.
D is wrong. Rate basis is a different question.
SC7-13  Answer A. 6,500,000 × 0.01 = 65,000.
B is wrong. That is ten times too large.
C is wrong. That is ten times too small.
D is wrong. That is the current interest charge.
SC7-14  Answer A. 1,600,000 × 0.75 = 1,200,000.
B is wrong. That applies the ratio before the natural offset.
C is wrong. That is the net exposure, unhedged.
D is wrong. That is the gross exposure.
SC7-15  Answer A. A mismatch is a position taken deliberately or otherwise.
B is wrong. A natural hedge arises from the business.
C is wrong. A limit is a rule.
D is wrong. A control is a procedure.
Your turn
@table
Situation | Method
Creator of a supplier may not approve its payment | Segregation of duties, a structural control.
Monthly report of duplicate invoice amounts | A detective control.
Buying packaging in the export currency | A natural hedge.
Thirteen weeks of receipts and payments | A rolling cash forecast.
A list of suppliers and systems a process needs | A critical dependency list, within continuity planning.
A 600,000 limit on one distributor | A concentration limit, within credit risk management.
What it tells you | Six methods, and only one of them involves buying anything. Most risk management is organisation, information and discipline rather than expenditure.
@end
