Case-style set: the new distributor
The credit exposure. Ninety-day terms on 2,400,000 of annual sales means a balance of about 2,400,000 ÷ 4 = 600,000 outstanding at any time, which is exactly the single-distributor tolerance and leaves no room for a late payment or a seasonal peak. The expected credit loss is 600,000 × 0.04 = 24,000 of defaulted exposure, of which 24,000 × 0.70 = 16,800 would be lost. Against a contribution of 2,400,000 × 0.40 = 960,000 a year that is small, so on expected value the trade is clearly worth doing.
The tolerances. The single-distributor limit is met exactly, which in practice means breached, since the balance will exceed 600,000 whenever payment runs late. The total export receivables limit of 2,500,000 must be checked against existing distributors before the limit is used. And the currency policy requires at least 75 per cent of the contracted euro exposure hedged.
The currency exposure. Euro receipts of 2,400,000 a year, less any euro costs already incurred, with each shipment exposed for ninety days — a long window, which is itself a risk management variable: the exposure is a function of the terms as much as of the rate.
Recommended terms. Four conditions, which together bring the proposal inside the tolerances.
Sixty-day terms rather than ninety, which cuts the standing balance to about 400,000 and leaves headroom under the 600,000 limit for a late payment.
A deposit or a documentary letter of credit for the first two shipments, after which the arrangement is reviewed on the payment record rather than on the agency rating.
Retention of title until payment, and a contractual right to suspend supply, so that the exposure can be stopped rather than merely watched.
And forward sales of euros covering 75 per cent of contracted receipts, dated to the expected receipt dates, with the natural offset of euro purchases taken first.
On those terms I would recommend acceptance. The contribution of 960,000 is large, the expected credit loss of 16,800 is a cost of doing business, and the two things that would make it unacceptable — a balance that breaches the limit and an unhedged currency position — are both fixed by the terms rather than by refusing the customer.
